import{_ as n,C as r,o as t,c as i,a2 as a,b as l,w as o,a as h,E as d,a3 as c}from"./chunks/framework.Ci_RTJJ5.js";const v=JSON.parse('{"title":"02 — Overview","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"chapters/02-tool-calling/overview.md","filePath":"chapters/02-tool-calling/overview.md"}'),u={name:"chapters/02-tool-calling/overview.md"};function p(m,e,g,q,f,b){const s=r("Mermaid");return t(),i("div",null,[e[1]||(e[1]=a('<h1 id="_02-—-overview" tabindex="-1">02 — Overview <a class="header-anchor" href="#_02-—-overview" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;02 — Overview&quot;">​</a></h1><p><em>How tool calling works, in general. Read this before you write code.</em></p><hr><h2 id="the-protocol-has-three-moves" tabindex="-1">The protocol has three moves <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-protocol-has-three-moves" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The protocol has three moves&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A model cannot read a file. It can only produce text. Tool calling works because both sides agree that certain text is a request:</p><ol><li><strong>Advertise.</strong> The request carries a list of tool schemas: a name, a description, and the shape of the arguments.</li><li><strong>Call.</strong> The model&#39;s reply contains a structured call instead of prose: an id, a name, and an arguments string.</li><li><strong>Return.</strong> The client runs the tool and appends a message that names the id it answers. Then it asks the model again.</li></ol>',6)),(t(),l(c,null,{default:o(()=>[d(s,{id:"mermaid-30",class:"mermaid",graph:"sequenceDiagram%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Agent%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Model%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20Tool%20as%20read%20tool%0A%20%20%20%20participant%20FS%20as%20workspace%0A%0A%20%20%20%20Agent-%3E%3EModel%3A%20messages%20%2B%20tool%20schemas%0A%20%20%20%20Model--%3E%3EAgent%3A%20tool_call%7Bid%3A%22call_1%22%2C%20name%3A%22read%22%2C%20arguments%3A'%7B%22path%22%3A%22a.txt%22%7D'%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Agent-%3E%3ETool%3A%20execute(arguments)%0A%20%20%20%20Tool-%3E%3EFS%3A%20open%20a.txt%0A%20%20%20%20FS--%3E%3ETool%3A%20contents%0A%20%20%20%20Tool--%3E%3EAgent%3A%20%22hello%22%0A%20%20%20%20Agent-%3E%3EModel%3A%20messages%20%2B%20assistant%20call%20%2B%20tool%20result%7Bid%3A%22call_1%22%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Model--%3E%3EAgent%3A%20%22The%20file%20says%20hello.%22%0A"})]),fallback:o(()=>[...e[0]||(e[0]=[h(" Loading... ",-1)])]),_:1})),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<p>The pairing is the contract. A tool result carries the id of the call it answers, and providers reject a conversation where a call has no result or a result has no call. Everything else in this chapter follows from that rule.</p><h2 id="why-the-arguments-arrive-in-pieces" tabindex="-1">Why the arguments arrive in pieces <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-arguments-arrive-in-pieces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the arguments arrive in pieces&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The reply streams, so the call streams with it. The first fragment for a call carries its index, id and name. Later fragments carry <strong>only the index and a piece of the arguments string</strong>:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>data: {&quot;choices&quot;:[{&quot;delta&quot;:{&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;index&quot;:0,&quot;id&quot;:&quot;call_1&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;function&quot;,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;read&quot;,&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;&quot;}}]}}]}</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>data: {&quot;choices&quot;:[{&quot;delta&quot;:{&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;index&quot;:0,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;{\\&quot;pa&quot;}}]}}]}</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>data: {&quot;choices&quot;:[{&quot;delta&quot;:{&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;index&quot;:0,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;th\\&quot;:\\&quot;a.&quot;}}]}}]}</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>data: {&quot;choices&quot;:[{&quot;delta&quot;:{&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;index&quot;:0,&quot;function&quot;:{&quot;arguments&quot;:&quot;txt\\&quot;}&quot;}}]}}]}</span></span></code></pre></div><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>  fragment 1:  index 0 · id call_1 · name read      ← identity</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>  fragment 2:  index 0 · arguments `{&quot;pa`           ← no id, no name</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>  fragment 3:  index 0 · arguments `th&quot;:&quot;a.`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>  fragment 4:  index 0 · arguments `txt&quot;}`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>                                                    ────────────────</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>  result:      one call, arguments `{&quot;path&quot;:&quot;a.txt&quot;}`</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Folding by <strong>index</strong> is the only reliable rule. Folding by id works until the second fragment arrives with an empty id, which is every real provider.</p><p>A model may also return several calls in one message. Each has its own index, and the results must be returned in the same order.</p><h2 id="failure-is-a-result-not-an-exception" tabindex="-1">Failure is a result, not an exception <a class="header-anchor" href="#failure-is-a-result-not-an-exception" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Failure is a result, not an exception&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The model&#39;s arguments are text it wrote, so they can be wrong. A path can be missing, a file can be huge, a path can climb out of the workspace, and the JSON itself can be malformed.</p><p>None of those should end the program. Each becomes a tool result with an error flag, so the model can read the message and try something else:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Result the model sees</th><th>Program</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>file missing</td><td><code>no such file: nope.txt</code></td><td>continues, exit 0</td></tr><tr><td>arguments malformed</td><td><code>arguments are not valid JSON: {&quot;path&quot;:</code></td><td>continues, exit 0</td></tr><tr><td>path escapes the workspace</td><td><code>path escapes the workspace: ../../etc/passwd</code></td><td>continues, exit 0</td></tr><tr><td>file larger than the limit</td><td>first 20,000 bytes + a truncation marker</td><td>continues, exit 0</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The exception is a broken request to the provider, which is not the model&#39;s fault and cannot be repaired by asking again.</p><h2 id="two-things-the-model-cannot-do" tabindex="-1">Two things the model cannot do <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-things-the-model-cannot-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two things the model cannot do&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>It cannot see the workspace.</strong> Everything it knows about a file comes from a tool result. That is why a truncation marker matters: without it, the model believes it read the whole file and will answer confidently about the part it never saw.</p><p><strong>It cannot recover from a silent failure.</strong> A tool that returns an empty string on error teaches the model that the file is empty. Say what happened.</p><h2 id="where-this-chapter-stops" tabindex="-1">Where this chapter stops <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-this-chapter-stops" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where this chapter stops&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This program makes <strong>one</strong> hop: call, run tools, ask once more, print. If the model asks for a second tool after the first result, the program prints whatever text came with that request and stops.</p><p>That limit is deliberate. Chapter 04 replaces the hop with a loop, and the difference between the two is easier to see when you have written the hop.</p><h2 id="where-the-real-implementations-go-further" tabindex="-1">Where the real implementations go further <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-real-implementations-go-further" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the real implementations go further&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>pi</th><th>crush</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parallel tool execution</td><td><code>packages/agent/src/agent-loop.ts</code></td><td><code>internal/agent/coordinator.go</code></td></tr><tr><td>Blocking a call before it runs</td><td><code>beforeToolCall</code> in <code>agent/types.ts</code></td><td><code>internal/permission/</code></td></tr><tr><td>Schema generation</td><td>TypeBox</td><td><code>internal/agent/agent_tool.go</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>They keep the same three moves. The extra code is policy, not protocol.</p><h2 id="self-check" tabindex="-1">Self-check <a class="header-anchor" href="#self-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Self-check&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Which field identifies a call, and which field groups its fragments? <em>(Why the arguments arrive in pieces)</em></li><li>A tool result is missing its <code>tool_call_id</code>. What does the provider do? <em>(The protocol has three moves)</em></li><li>Why does a truncation marker change the model&#39;s answer quality? <em>(Two things the model cannot do)</em></li><li>Name three failures that must become results rather than exits. <em>(Failure is a result, not an exception)</em></li><li>What does the program do if the second reply also asks for a tool? <em>(Where this chapter stops)</em></li></ol>',23))])}const x=n(u,[["render",p]]);export{v as __pageData,x as default};
